class Solution {
    public int subarraySum(int[] nums, int k) {
        // Initialise variables and hashmap
        Map<Integer, Integer> hm = new HashMap<>();
        int sum = 0, count = 0;

        //base case : if the ele at 0th idx == sum 
        // we can already have a freq
        hm.put(0,1); 

        for(int num : nums){
            //Prefix sum
            sum += num;

            // if i = sum - k exist
            // we increase the count by getting the freq of sum - K
            if(hm.containsKey(sum - k)) {
                count = count + hm.get(sum-k);
            }

            hm.put(sum, hm.getOrDefault(sum,0)+1);
        }

        return count;
    }
}
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity: O(N)**

You traverse the array once.

Each step performs:

Constant‑time arithmetic (sum += nums[i])

Constant‑time hashmap lookup (hm.get(sum - k))

Constant‑time hashmap update (hm.put(sum, ...))

No nested loops, no extra passes.

So total operations scale linearly with the number of elements.

**Space Complexity:**
O(n) in the worst case.

The hashmap stores prefix sums.

In the worst case, every prefix sum is unique.

That means the hashmap grows to size n.

